# ============================================================================
# GENERIC FIRMWARE — kept, NOT Sky1-specific (real WiFi/BT cards found on
# generic AMD64 laptops and desktops too, same package names in Debian).
# ============================================================================
# 2026-09-20: firmware-linux-free above is the FREE-licensed subset only --
# firmware-linux (no suffix) is the broader non-free-inclusive metapackage
# covering additional real hardware Debian ships firmware for that has no
# individually-named package here (older/misc chipsets). Real gap found
# during a comprehensive out-of-box hardware coverage pass, distinct from
# the b43/AX201/RTL8168h chip-specific gaps found the same night.
# 2026-09-20: Broadcom wired-NIC firmware (BCM577xx/578xx family, common on
# business/enterprise laptops and servers) -- firmware-brcm80211 above only
# covers Broadcom WiFi, not this separate wired-ethernet firmware family.
# Same class of gap as the b43/AX201/RTL8168h findings the same night.
# Complete firmware seed: manifests/firmware.pkgs.
# REMOVED 2026-09-22: firmware-b43-installer / firmware-b43legacy-installer
# (added 2026-09-18, see below for the original reasoning -- still valid
# as a hardware-coverage argument, but the package can't actually build
# through this pipeline). Its postinst needs to fetch the firmware from
# Broadcom's site at package-configure time, which the mkosi sandboxed
# apt install has no route to (real build failure, confirmed on ACHILLES
# 2026-09-22: `dpkg` exit 1, "Errors were encountered while processing:
# firmware-b43-installer / firmware-b43legacy-installer"). These are
# pre-~2012 Intel Mac WiFi chips -- not an NCZ-OS target platform (Sky1,
# T2 Macs via brcmfmac, or modern AMD/nvidia amd64) -- so dropping rather
# than solving the sandboxed-fetch problem for hardware this build
# doesn't actually ship to.
#
# Original 2026-09-18 note, for context: "older (pre-~2012) Intel Macs use
# b43/b43legacy-class Broadcom WiFi, distinct from the brcmfmac-class chips
# firmware-brcm80211 already covers (2012+ Macs, already confirmed
# working). b43 firmware isn't Debian-redistributable -- same caveat class
# as the T2 WiFi firmware fetch. Real gap, never previously evaluated (not
# a deliberate DROPPED-section exclusion) -- confirmed via kernel .config
# audit (CONFIG_B43/CONFIG_B43LEGACY both present) that the driver side
# was already covered, only the firmware package was missing."
# 2026-09-17: confirmed present on the real, official Debian 13.7.0 amd64
# DVD-1 "with firmware" pool/non-free-firmware -- AMD iGPU firmware, real
# gap (many laptops pair a discrete GPU with an AMD APU's integrated one,
# e.g. Ryzen-based machines like the one this gap was found on).

# --- x86-specific microcode (arm64 has no equivalent; rescue.pkgs' arm64
#     header explicitly excluded these as "not applicable to a headless
#     arm64 box" — they ARE applicable here) -------------------------------
intel-microcode
amd64-microcode

# --- T2-CONDITIONAL firmware — installed only when the hardware-detection
#     hook (see docs/AMD64-PACKAGE-AUDIT.md) identifies a real Apple T2
#     machine, NOT unconditionally on every AMD64 install. Real source
#     confirmed 2026-09-14: github.com/AdityaGarg8/Apple-Firmware ships an
#     actual architecture-independent Debian package
#     (apple-firmware_14.5-1_all.deb) carrying the T2/M1-era Broadcom
#     WiFi/BT blobs. Independently cross-checked live on MEDUSA itself,
#     which already has the AUR equivalent (apple-bcm-firmware) installed —
#     hundreds of real files at /usr/lib/firmware/brcm/*apple*, one per
#     T2/M1 Broadcom chip variant, confirmed present on real hardware.
#     NOT YET WIRED into an install-time hook — see follow-on work in the
#     audit doc. Listed here as the confirmed real package to pull in, not a
#     guess.
# apple-firmware   [T2-conditional — do not uncomment unconditionally]

# ============================================================================
# GPU/ACCELERATOR STACK — install-time hardware-detected, NOT unconditional.
# Real package names verified against Debian forky:
# ============================================================================
#   AMD (PCI vendor 1002/1022)  → rocm-opencl-icd, rocm-smi, rocminfo,
#                                  rocm-device-libs-21, libamdhip64-6, hipcc,
#                                  rocm-cmake (Debian packages; no
#                                  third-party repo needed for ROCm itself)
#   NVIDIA (PCI vendor 10de)    → nvidia-driver (Debian non-free-firmware
#                                  metapackage) for the driver; full CUDA
#                                  toolkit needs NVIDIA's own apt repo
#                                  (developer.download.nvidia.com/compute/
#                                  cuda/repos/debian13/x86_64/ — already
#                                  precedented live on CERBERUS itself)
#   Intel (PCI vendor 8086)     → intel-media-va-driver (VAAPI, real Debian
#                                  package). Full OpenVINO toolkit is NOT in
#                                  Debian's archive (confirmed absent via
#                                  live apt-cache search) — needs Intel's own
#                                  repo (apt.repos.intel.com), same class of
#                                  gap as CUDA above.
#
# INTERIM (2026-09-29), pending the vendor-probe hook described below.
# Ship the Intel VAAPI driver unconditionally on amd64. Every amd64 machine
# this project supports has an Intel iGPU -- the T2 Macs carry UHD 630
# (8086:3e9b) and PEGASUS a CometLake UHD (8086:9bc4) -- so there is no
# machine where this is dead weight, and waiting for the probe framework
# left real hardware with NO video acceleration at all.
#
# MEASURED on PEGASUS 2026-09-29:
#     vainfo: va_openDriver() returns -1
#       (tried iHD_drv_video.so, then i965_drv_video.so)
#     VAAPI profiles: 0        va-driver packages installed: 0
# versus 19 profiles on the T2 Macs, which get radeonsi VAAPI from mesa via
# the dGPU. PEGASUS therefore decodes all video on the CPU -- the same class
# of problem as the thermal complaint that started this, on a machine whose
# RTX 2060 and iGPU can both do it in hardware.
intel-media-va-driver

# Design (NOT YET IMPLEMENTED — see docs/AMD64-PACKAGE-AUDIT.md follow-on
# work): an installer hook probes `lspci -nn` for PCI vendor IDs 10de/1002/
# 1022/8086 and installs only the matching stack(s) — a machine can have more
# than one (e.g. Intel iGPU + NVIDIA dGPU), so this is additive-per-vendor-
# found, not a single exclusive choice. None of these packages are seeded
# unconditionally in this manifest; they are installed by that future hook,
# analogous to how post-install/12-sky1-firmware.sh and
# post-install/53-chrome.sh already do conditional/staged installs on the
# Sky1 side.
